德怀特对此似乎很不高兴。

158
00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:25.960
He did.
确实。

159
00:07:26.140 --> 00:07:28.640
And when we get to that, Angela, I have a catch.
当我们谈到这一点时，安吉拉，我有个小插曲。

160
00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:29.240
Okay.
好的。

161
00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:29.900
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

162
00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:33.580
Speaking of catches, are you ready for Fast Fact 3?
说到插曲，你准备好迎接第三个“速查事实”了吗？

163
00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:35.780
I don't know if I can.
我不知道我能不能行。

164
00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:41.900
Well, this is a Florida fan catch fact check from Aaron W.
好吧，这是一条来自新墨西哥州亚伦·W的佛罗里达粉丝事实核查。

165
00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:42.860
in New Mexico.
再说一遍。

166
00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:43.740
Say that again.
再说一遍。

167
00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:46.540
A Florida fan catch fact check?
佛罗里达粉丝事实核查？

168
00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:47.120
Yes.
是的。

169
00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:47.820
Oy yoy yoy.
哎哟喂。

170
00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:48.960
Fan catch fact check.
粉丝事实核查。

171
00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:49.420
Okay.
好吧。

172
00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:52.240
Here is what Aaron had to say.
这是亚伦要说的话。

173
00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:54.380
I am a native Floridian.
我是土生土长的佛罗里达人。

174
00:07:54.660 --> 00:08:00.260
I grew up in Miami until I was 18, then moved to Tallahassee to attend Florida State University
我在迈阿密长大，直到18岁，然后搬到塔拉哈西就读佛罗里达州立大学，

175
00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:04.820
and ended up living there for 11 years until I moved to New Mexico in late 2020.
最后在那里住了11年，直到2020年底搬到新墨西哥州。

176
00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:11.480
I noticed that all of Dunder Mifflin thinks that all of Florida is the same, and they assume
我注意到邓德·米夫林的所有人都认为整个佛罗里达都一样，他们以为

177
00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:13.460
Tallahassee will be just like Miami.
塔拉哈西会和迈阿密一模一样。

178
00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:16.160
But let me tell you, the two could not be more different.
但让我告诉你，这两者简直天差地别。

179
00:08:17.420 --> 00:08:20.740
Tallahassee is a medium-sized, quiet, southern college town.
塔拉哈西是一个中等规模、安静的南方大学城。

180
00:08:20.740 --> 00:08:24.980
There are giant oak trees with Spanish moss and hardly any palm trees.
那里有巨大的橡树，挂着西班牙苔藓，几乎没有棕榈树。

181
00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:30.020
And while there aren't four distinct seasons, Tallahassee can get very cold in the winter
虽然那里没有四季分明，但塔拉哈西的冬季月份

182
00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:30.420
months.
可能会非常冷。

183
00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:35.000
So here are just a few things I noticed in Special Project that made me think Dunder
所以，我在《特别项目》中注意到了一些事情，让我觉得邓德·

184
00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:40.060
Mifflin assumes all of Florida is the same or that they will be actually in Miami.
米夫林认为整个佛罗里达都一样，或者他们以为自己真的在迈阿密。

185
00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:43.620
First, Dwight tells Pam to pack swimwear.
首先，德怀特让帕姆带上泳装。

186
00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:47.280
February in Tallahassee is not pool or beach weather.
塔拉哈西的二月根本不是游泳或去海滩的天气。

187
00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:51.000
The closest beach to Tallahassee is almost 40 miles away.
离塔拉哈西最近的海滩也有近40英里远。

188
00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:55.740
I guess the average temperature around Valentine's Day would be in the 60s.
我猜情人节前后的平均气温大概在60多华氏度（约15-20摄氏度）。

189
00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:59.860
Next, Dwight mentions trips to Cape Canaveral.
其次，德怀特提到了去卡纳维拉尔角旅行。

190
00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:03.740
That is 309 miles away from Tallahassee.
那离塔拉哈西有309英里远。

191
00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:04.100
Oh, dear.
天哪。

192
00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:10.580
He mentions Gloria Estefan, who lives in Miami, which is 482 miles away.
他提到了住在迈阿密的格洛丽亚·伊斯特芬，那可是482英里远。

193
00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:16.920
Then, Dwight mentions it is 85 degrees Fahrenheit with 73% humidity.
然后，德怀特提到气温是85华氏度（约29摄氏度），湿度73%。

194
00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:21.680
Again, I understand he's trying to convince people not to go, but this weather is true
再说一次，我明白他是想劝大家别去，但这种天气只适合

195
00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:23.720
for late spring summer, not February.
春末夏初，而不是二月。

196
00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:29.360
Also, hurricane season does not officially begin until June 1st, and it runs to November 30th.
此外，飓风季节正式开始于6月1日，一直持续到11月30日。

197
00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:32.700
Clearly, the writers did not consult anyone from Florida.
显然，编剧们根本没咨询过任何佛罗里达人。

198
00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:34.160
I know.
我知道。

199
00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:37.100
As I was reading this letter, I was like, wow.
当我读这封信的时候，我心想，哇。

200
00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:38.160
Wow.
哇。

201
00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:43.640
The people living in Tallahassee who are watching our episode right now just must be squirming.
现在正在看我们这一集的塔拉哈西居民肯定坐立难安了。

202
00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:47.160
Although this is Dwight's perception of Florida, right?
虽然这只是德怀特眼中的佛罗里达，对吧？

203
00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:47.600
True.
没错。

204
00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:48.180
Yeah.
是啊。

205
00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:49.880
Which may be tracks.
这可能也是事实。

206
00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:54.500
Erin did say that giant cockroaches, a.k.a.
艾琳确实说过那种巨型蟑螂，也就是所谓的

207
00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:56.580
palmetto bugs, do not Google this,
帕尔梅托虫，别去谷歌搜这个，

208
00:09:56.740 --> 00:10:01.520
Erin said, are sadly accurate, and they fly.
艾琳说，这描述很准确，而且它们会飞。

209
00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:02.920
I know they fly.
我知道它们会飞。

210
00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:06.360
I've had one fly at me, a cockroach.
我就被蟑螂扑过。

211
00:10:06.460 --> 00:10:11.120
It's just maybe the most, like, undone I've ever come.
那可能是我这辈子最失态的一次了。

212
00:10:11.260 --> 00:10:12.240
Like, you would have thought.
真的，你肯定想不到。

213
00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:13.060
I was like, ah!
我当时就，啊！

214
00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:14.100
I went crazy.
我整个人都疯了。

215
00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:16.920
Yeah, I don't like bugs that fly at me.
是啊，我不喜欢会朝我飞过来的虫子。

216
00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:20.380
Not big, hard-shell, crunchy ones, either.
尤其是那种又大、壳又硬、还会发出嘎吱声的。

217
00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:21.040
That's right.
没错。

218
00:10:21.340 --> 00:10:22.760
I don't like that.
我不喜欢那样。

219
00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:23.340
Don't like.
不喜欢。

220
00:10:23.720 --> 00:10:27.900
Finally, Erin said mosquitoes and giant frogs are also accurate.
最后，艾琳说蚊子和巨型青蛙的描述也很准确。

221
00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:29.520
Okay.
好吧。

222
00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:30.660
Yeah.
是啊。

223
00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:31.220
Okay.
好吧。

224
00:10:32.020 --> 00:10:35.000
Well, Erin, thank you very much for the Tallahassee breakdown.
那么，艾琳，非常感谢你对塔拉哈西的详细解读。

225
00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:35.840
That's right.
没错。

226
00:10:36.460 --> 00:10:41.860
Well, now that we've set the record straight on maternity leave and Florida.
好了，既然我们已经澄清了产假和佛罗里达的情况。

227
00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:45.240
Well, maternity leave in Tallahassee.
嗯，是塔拉哈西的产假。

228
00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:46.100
Yes.
是的。

229
00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:47.660
Florida's big.
佛罗里达很大。

230
00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:49.220
We don't know all about Florida.
我们并不了解佛罗里达的一切。

231
00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:50.680
That was Erin's point.
这就是艾琳的观点。

232
00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:52.380
Yes, I agree, Erin.
是的，我同意，艾琳。

233
00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:56.820
Let's take a break, and when we come back, it's Valentine's Day.
我们休息一下，回来后就是情人节了。

234
00:10:56.820 --> 00:10:57.940
We'll get into it.
我们到时候再细聊。

235
00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:10.690
We are back, and we're at front reception.
我们回来了，现在在前台。

236
00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:13.730
It's very decorated for Valentine's.
这里为了情人节做了很多装饰。

237
00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:18.210
Sorry I didn't do a Cupid breakdown, but I have some other stuff.
抱歉我没做丘比特的详细解读，但我还有别的东西。

238
00:11:18.650 --> 00:11:19.930
Pam is going to return.
帕姆要回来了。

239
00:11:19.930 --> 00:11:21.730
And everyone's very happy to see her.
大家都很高兴见到她。

240
00:11:22.430 --> 00:11:25.610
Stanley kind of hints that she's just had an eight-week vacation.
斯坦利暗示她刚度了八周的假。

241
00:11:26.270 --> 00:11:28.490
Pam's like, oh, it wasn't really a vacation.
帕姆说，哦，那其实不算度假。

242
00:11:29.130 --> 00:11:33.770
Angela's also going to return after her three-day maternity leave, as we talked about.
安吉拉也要在休完我们之前提到的三天产假后回来了。

243
00:11:33.770 --> 00:11:38.090
And this outfit was a whole meeting, I can tell you that.
而且这套服装可是开过专门会议决定的，我可以告诉你。

244
00:11:38.470 --> 00:11:38.830
Really?
真的吗？

245
00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:39.410
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

246
00:11:39.650 --> 00:11:40.230
Say more.
多说说看。

247
00:11:40.550 --> 00:11:45.350
They really wanted to sell this idea that she came back as if nothing had happened.
他们真的很想传达出一种她回来时就像什么都没发生过的感觉。

248
00:11:45.650 --> 00:11:45.970
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

249
00:11:46.390 --> 00:11:47.510
I had multiple fittings.
我试穿了好几次。

250
00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:51.510
They wanted everything to be tight-fitted and very svelte.
他们想要一切都紧身且显得非常苗条。

251
00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:52.310
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

252
00:11:52.790 --> 00:11:55.010
The belt was a whole conversation.
那条腰带简直是个大话题。

253
00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:55.790
Oh.
哦。

254
00:11:56.010 --> 00:11:58.790
I don't know if you noticed, but I have a tight little turtleneck,
不知道你有没有注意到，我穿了一件紧身小高领毛衣，

255
00:11:58.850 --> 00:12:01.910
and then I have the tiny skinny belt right at my waiSt.
然后腰间系了一条细细的小腰带。

256
00:12:01.930 --> 00:12:02.550
I did notice that.
我注意到了。

257
00:12:02.550 --> 00:12:03.530
And a pencil skirt.
还有一条铅笔裙。

258
00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:05.330
Everyone wanted the belt.
每个人都想要那条腰带。

259
00:12:05.630 --> 00:12:06.690
I was like, do we really need the belt?
我当时想，我们真的需要那条腰带吗？

260
00:12:06.770 --> 00:12:08.190
They're like, you need the belt.
他们说，你需要那条腰带。

261
00:12:08.310 --> 00:12:12.050
Because I guess the belt really accentuates the waiSt.
因为我想那条腰带确实能凸显腰身。

262
00:12:12.370 --> 00:12:18.290
You know what I thought when I saw the Angela from maternity leave and Pam from maternity leave?
你知道当我看到产假归来的安吉拉和产假归来的帕姆时，我在想什么吗？

263
00:12:18.490 --> 00:12:22.970
I thought Angela is Instagram, and Pam is real life.
我在想安吉拉是Instagram上的样子，而帕姆是现实生活中的样子。

264
00:12:23.170 --> 00:12:23.850
That trend.
那种趋势。

265
00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:24.450
Yes.
没错。

266
00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:24.950
Yeah.
是啊。

267
00:12:25.070 --> 00:12:29.910
Like, Angela is what postpartum moms on Instagram are showing you.
就像安吉拉代表了Instagram上那些产后妈妈展示给你的样子。

268
00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:30.850
They're snapback.
她们恢复得很快。

269
00:12:31.030 --> 00:12:31.210
Yeah.
是啊。

270
00:12:31.210 --> 00:12:36.750
And then Pam is, like, at home being like, what the F is happening?
而帕姆就像是在家里想，这到底是怎么回事？

271
00:12:37.430 --> 00:12:41.250
Well, in addition to her outfit, she's also brought in a lot of snacks.
嗯，除了她的装扮，她还带了很多零食。

272
00:12:42.050 --> 00:12:43.790
Brownies and cookie bars.
布朗尼和饼干棒。

273
00:12:43.790 --> 00:12:45.930
Brownies with and without walnuts.
有核桃的和没核桃的布朗尼。

274
00:12:46.310 --> 00:12:46.450
Yeah.
是啊。

275
00:12:46.670 --> 00:12:48.290
Because she does not have mommy brain.
因为她没有“妈妈脑”。

276
00:12:48.470 --> 00:12:48.590
Yeah.
是啊。

277
00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:49.950
She doesn't know what Pam's talking about.
她不知道帕姆在说什么。

278
00:12:50.890 --> 00:12:54.410
Angela, I remember filming this moment where you offer me a brownie,
安吉拉，我记得拍这一幕时，你递给我一块布朗尼，

279
00:12:54.490 --> 00:12:56.910
and I have to decide if I'm going to take it.
我得决定要不要吃。

280
00:12:56.910 --> 00:12:59.490
And it kept making us laugh so hard.
这让我们一直笑个不停。

281
00:12:59.630 --> 00:13:04.190
Not only did it make us laugh, but then after you take a bite and you like the brownie.
不仅让我们笑，而且在你咬了一口觉得布朗尼好吃之后。

282
00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:04.590
I know.
我知道。

283
00:13:04.810 --> 00:13:06.530
Then you're like, what about you?
然后你说，你呢？

284
00:13:06.590 --> 00:13:07.310
Don't you want one?
你不来一块吗？

285
00:13:07.470 --> 00:13:09.550
And I'm like, I'm trying to watch my weight.
我说，我正在控制体重。

286
00:13:09.930 --> 00:13:10.630
I know.
我知道。

287
00:13:10.990 --> 00:13:13.370
It was making us laugh so hard.
这真的让我们笑得不行。

288
00:13:13.750 --> 00:13:14.970
The brownies were good.
那些布朗尼很好吃。

289
00:13:15.330 --> 00:13:16.530
They were very good.
非常好吃。

290
00:13:16.530 --> 00:13:20.950
But this also made me think of, you know what I was eating at this time?
但这也让我想起，你知道我当时在吃什么吗？

291
00:13:21.210 --> 00:13:21.450
What?
什么？

292
00:13:21.570 --> 00:13:23.710
I was eating those lactation cookies.
我在吃那种催乳饼干。

293
00:13:24.110 --> 00:13:25.270
Did you make those?
是你自己做的吗？

294
00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:28.530
They were all the rage when I was postpartum.
我产后那会儿这东西特别流行。

295
00:13:28.670 --> 00:13:33.530
They were like an oatmeal cookie that you made with brewer's yeast and coconut oil,
它们就像是用啤酒酵母和椰子油做的燕麦饼干，

296
00:13:33.530 --> 00:13:36.150
and they were supposed to help you with your milk production.
据说能帮你增加奶水。

297
00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:40.710
And so I was eating these cookies all the time.
所以我当时一直在吃这些饼干。

298
00:13:40.890 --> 00:13:40.990
Right, right.
对，没错。

299
00:13:41.030 --> 00:13:43.910
So it was really good to eat that chocolate brownie.
所以能吃上一块巧克力布朗尼真是太棒了。

300
00:13:44.210 --> 00:13:45.130
Like a break from those?
就像是换换口味？

301
00:13:45.310 --> 00:13:45.470
Yeah.
是啊。

302
00:13:45.470 --> 00:13:49.170
What I remembered most about this scene is how tickled we got.
关于这一幕，我印象最深的就是我们当时笑得有多开心。

303
00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:52.650
And it was you and I having the dialogue just between the two of us.
而且那场戏只有我们俩在对话。

304
00:13:52.750 --> 00:13:55.110
So that meant everyone was waiting for us to finish our lines.
这意味着所有人都得等着我们把台词说完。

305
00:13:55.150 --> 00:13:56.510
And we kept laughing.
可我们一直笑个不停。

306
00:13:56.990 --> 00:14:00.370
And I am telling you, you can see John check out.
我跟你说，你都能看出约翰已经出戏了。

307
00:14:01.050 --> 00:14:05.950
If you look at his face, he's just like, oh my God, are they going to get through this?
如果你看他的表情，他就像在说，天哪，她们到底能不能演完啊？

308
00:14:06.070 --> 00:14:06.990
It's so true.
确实如此。

309
00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:11.890
Well, this cold open ends with Pam trying to give cash to people.
嗯，这一段开场戏以帕姆试图给人们现金结束。

310
00:14:12.290 --> 00:14:12.570
Yeah.
是啊。

311
00:14:13.370 --> 00:14:14.970
And Jim's like, no, it's done.
吉姆说，不，不用了。

312
00:14:14.970 --> 00:14:15.450
Stop.
停下。

313
00:14:15.690 --> 00:14:15.830
It's done.
不用了。

314
00:14:16.130 --> 00:14:16.180
It's done.
不用了。

315
00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:16.630
Forget it.
算了吧。

316
00:14:16.790 --> 00:14:18.450
You didn't bring in any brownies.
你又没带布朗尼蛋糕来。

317
00:14:18.790 --> 00:14:19.610
It's fine.
